{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>You stretched out Your right hand, etc.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said in the Sifra (Mekhilta) that the sea would fling them onto the dry land and the dry land back into the sea. The dry land said to the sea: ״If in a place where I received only the blood of a single Hevel I was told ׳אֲרוּרָה׳ etc.,׳ now how can I receive this entire multitude?״ — until the Holy One, blessed be He, swore to it that He would not put it on trial, as it is written ״נָטִיתָ יְמִינְךָ״ etc. — end of quotation. And so Targum Yonatan ben Uziel translated. From here you see that the sea did not wish to receive them and was spitting them out onto the dry land. Yet we do not find this in what they said in Pesaḥim 118, and this is their language: The Holy One, blessed be He, said to the prince of the sea, ״Spit them out onto the dry land.״ He said before Him: ״Master of the universe, is there a slave whose master gives him a gift and then takes it back from him?״ He said to him: ״I will give you one and a half״ etc. He said to Him: ״Is there a slave who makes demands of his master?״ He said to him: ״The Kishon brook shall be your guarantor.״ Immediately he spat them out onto the dry land, and Israel came and saw them, as it is said ״וַיַּרְא יִשְׂרָאֵל״ etc. — end of quotation. Now, they of blessed memory explicitly stated that the sea was not willing to spit them out. If so, afterward when He returned them to the earth, why did it not rejoice to receive them? Also, why was HaShem required to pay it back from the camp of Sisera — behold, it is before you: He could have returned them to it on its own and there would have been no need for a guarantor?" "<b>Further,</b> it is difficult, for our eyes see that the sea does not tolerate a person or beast within it but spits them out onto the dry land — I recall several times that people drowned in the sea and it cast them onto the dry land, some on that very day, some after a day or two. And according to their words of blessed memory, the sea wanted them to remain with it. And I saw that Rashi and Rashbam explained that the sea's claim was on account of the sustenance of the fish of the sea, that they would find food. Now one needs to know: if so, why did it not receive them when the dry land flung them and they would have been food for the fish? And it would appear possible to answer that at that point they were putrid and unfit for fish to eat — but this is a forced answer, for presumably HaShem would not delay telling the dry land to bury them on account of the law of ״לֹא תָלִין״ (Deuteronomy 21:23), and besides, there is no need in this matter to delay their shame upon the face of the earth except until they die — and after death, what need is there to delay without burial?" "And although it is not our way in this composition to explain the sayings of our Rabbis of blessed memory, nevertheless this matter is necessary for understanding the verse, and so that we not call them contradictory midrashim, it appears fitting to reconcile them according to their statement of blessed memory (Mekhilta on Exodus 14:30), which we wrote there at the verse ״וַיַּרְא יִשְׂרָאֵל etc. מֵת עַל שְׂפַת הַיָּם״ — that they saw them at the point of death while still somewhat alive, so that they would recognize them, and then etc. According to these words of theirs, light shone upon both sayings, for what the sea asked — that it not spit them out — was because HaShem had told it to spit them out alive, for the reason mentioned, to gladden His beloved that the Egyptians would recognize Israel and see and be covered with shame, as stated in their words regarding the magnitude of this miracle. And although the matter of the spitting out being while they were yet alive is not explicitly hinted at in their saying, even so our Rabbis of blessed memory rely on what is known among them. And therefore the sea protested ״Is there a slave who gives״ etc. — the intent being that it wished to complete the mitzvah against those who provoke El. And you will find that all the celestial princes and all creatures above and below desire and yearn to perform the mitzvot of HaShem, for they are needy in this regard since they have no mitzvot. And so you will find there in Pesaḥim in that same sugya, that Gabriel asked HaShem to save Abraham from the fiery furnace, and they said there that HaShem did not shortchange his reward and said to him, ״You will merit to save three of his grandsons״ etc. — end of quotation. See that it is a merit for angels to do kindness for the righteous and lovers of the Most High. And likewise the sea wished that the departure of their souls would be completed in the sea and not on the dry land, and therefore its heart cried out, and El Most High answered it in the manner He did for Gabriel — that in the future He would provide it with another merit to destroy a wicked people, a half-portion, namely the camp of Sisera, upon whom it would perform the mitzvah of destroying the wicked." "<b>Further,</b> there is another reason for the sea's claim — that it wished their death to be completed in the sea in order to take all the vital forces, which are all the souls of that people, for it is the nature of the souls of the nations to remain in the place of their death, as it says (Ecclesiastes 3:19) ״וְרוּחַ הַבְּהֵמָה הַיּוֹרֶדֶת הִיא לְמַטָּה לָאָרֶץ״, and idol worshippers are likened to beasts. And when their souls would depart in the sea, the prince would merit through them [to draw] the spiritual perceptions that come to it from this matter. And therefore when He told it to spit them out alive — at which point the earth would gain the matter, for they acquire their place at the location where they are laid — its heart cried out ״Is there a slave״ etc., until its master answered it that He would give it in exchange for them etc. — meaning, human beings alive like these. And He specified for him equivalents to these, and it would draw vitality from them — which is the essence of its desire, not from the body. And therefore after it spat them out and their souls departed at the edge of the sea and the earth flung them into the sea, it did not wish to receive them, for it is not in its nature [to receive the dead]; rather the earth is what covers over its children who were taken from it, and HaShem forced it to receive them, for the matter concerns it." "And according to our approach it should not be difficult from what they said there in Pesaḥim (Pesaḥim 118b), that when HaShem paid back the sea, the fish in the sea opened and said ״וֶאֱמֶת ה׳ לְעוֹלָם״ — end of quotation; if so, the matter concerns them. And perhaps it is because at the time when the sea desires to receive them within it — which is when they are alive — the fish would benefit from them in the interim before they die, and afterward the sea would cast them out and they would no longer have food from them, and it was concerning the time of their sustenance that they said song." "And I have seen someone who took offense at the sea's argument that ״there is a slave״ and so forth — for is not the hand of a slave like the hand of his master, and there is no grounds for complaint here, since the sea's argument is correct, in accordance with the saying (Ta'anit 25a): מִשָּׁמַיִם מֵיהַב יָהֲבֵי, מִשְׁקַל לָא שָׁקְלֵי — He speaks on the authority of his master's mastery. And has such a thing never happened to any slave among the slaves of HaShem, that his master gave him something and then took it back, and so forth? And therefore he did not say ״there is a master who gives and so forth and takes back״ and so forth, for that is not an argument at all — there is indeed [such a case], and moreover the hand of a slave is like the hand of his master, as though he had never given it at all; rather, his [the slave's] possessions belong to his master, for he does not conduct himself thus with his slaves."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {5 "The Aramaic phrase from Ta'anit 25a is left in original script as Or HaChaim quotes it; it means roughly 'from Heaven He gave a gift, [but] did not weigh [measure] it out' — i.e., what is given from Above is not truly relinquished."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:28:59.481468268Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}